  "limitations": [
    "Participants were young healthy adults, potentially leading to selection bias and limited generalizability.",
    "No prior information on variability of physiological and cognitive responses for formal power-sample size calculations.",
    "Study included only two conditions (low BIDI score = 3, moderate BIDI score = 27), suggesting potentially greater impacts with more biophilic components.",
    "Study conducted in a building with good air quality and thermal comfort, preventing parsing of effects to any single natural element or interactions with other IEQ factors.",
    "Despite efforts to minimize differences, baseline physiological measures differed between visits, potentially affecting physiological and cognitive changes.",
    "Participants were not blinded to the test environments, potentially leading to biased estimates of effects."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This randomized crossover study investigated the physiological and cognitive responses to biophilic indoor environments compared to non-biophilic settings, both in reality and via virtual reality (VR). Findings indicate that short exposure to a biophilic indoor environment was associated with significant decreases in systolic and diastolic blood pressure, and skin conductance level, alongside improved short-term memory and more positive emotions (engagement, excitement) and fewer negative emotions (stress, frustration). Notably, VR exposure to biophilic environments evoked similar physiological and cognitive responses as actual physical exposure. Limitations include a young, healthy participant sample, limited biophilic conditions, and potential for unblinded participant bias.",
